<subsection xml:id="subsec-inhomogeneous-higher-order">
  <title>Inhomogeneous Linear ODEs — Method of Undetermined Coefficients</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      We now turn to the <em>inhomogeneous</em> linear ODE with constant
      coefficients:
      <me>
        y^{(n)}(t) + a_{n-1}\, y^{(n-1)}(t) + \cdots + a_1\, y'(t)
        + a_0\, y(t) = f(t), \qquad f(t) \not\equiv 0.
      </me>
      The crucial structural fact is the <em>superposition principle</em>:
      the general solution is the sum of the general homogeneous solution
      (which we already know how to find) and <em>any</em> particular
      solution of the inhomogeneous equation.
    </p>
  </introduction>

  <!-- ─── Superposition principle ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-superposition-principle">
    <title>Superposition Principle</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>y_p(t)</m> be any particular solution of the inhomogeneous ODE
        <me>
          y^{(n)} + a_{n-1} y^{(n-1)} + \cdots + a_0 y = f(t).
        </me>
        Let <m>y_h(t) = C_1 y_1(t) + \cdots + C_n y_n(t)</m> be the general
        solution of the corresponding <em>homogeneous</em> ODE (i.e.\ with
        <m>f \equiv 0</m>).
        Then the <term>general solution of the inhomogeneous ODE</term> is
        <me>
          y(t) = y_h(t) + y_p(t),
        </me>
        and every solution of the inhomogeneous ODE has this form for some
        choice of <m>C_1, \ldots, C_n \in \R</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <!-- ─── Three-step method ─── -->

  <!-- ADDED: checklist format makes the workflow explicit and avoids fragile algorithm markup -->
  <assemblage xml:id="alg-undetermined-coefficients">
    <title>Method of Undetermined Coefficients</title>
    <p>
      For the forcing terms covered in this section, use the following
      three-step workflow.
    </p>
    <ol>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Solve the homogeneous ODE.</em>
          Find <m>y_h(t) = C_1 y_1(t) + \cdots + C_n y_n(t)</m> using the
          characteristic polynomial (see
          <xref ref="alg-solve-homogeneous"/>).
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Guess the form of a particular solution <m>y_p(t)</m></em>
          based on the right-hand side <m>f(t)</m>, using the table
          in <xref ref="thm-undetermined-coefficient-guesses"/>.
          Then substitute <m>y_p</m> into the ODE and solve for the
          undetermined coefficients.
        </p>
      </li>
      <li>
        <p>
          <em>Write the general solution:</em>
          <m>y(t) = y_h(t) + y_p(t)</m>.
          Apply initial conditions to determine <m>C_1, \ldots, C_n</m>.
        </p>
      </li>
    </ol>
  </assemblage>

  <!-- ─── Guess table ─── -->

  <theorem xml:id="thm-undetermined-coefficient-guesses">
    <title>Educated Guesses for the Particular Solution</title>
    <statement>
      <p>
        Let <m>P(\lambda)</m> be the characteristic polynomial of the
        homogeneous ODE.
        The following table gives the standard ansatz for <m>y_p(t)</m>
        depending on the form of <m>f(t)</m>.
        In each row, <m>k</m> denotes the multiplicity of the relevant
        frequency as a root of <m>P</m> (set <m>k = 0</m> if it is not a
        root).
      </p>
      <tabular halign="left" top="medium">
        <col right="medium"/>
        <col right="medium"/>
        <col/>
        <row header="yes" bottom="medium">
          <cell>Right-hand side <m>f(t)</m></cell>
          <cell>Ansatz for <m>y_p(t)</m></cell>
          <cell>Resonance condition</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell>Polynomial <m>\displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{m} A_j t^j</m></cell>
          <cell><m>t^k \displaystyle\sum_{j=0}^{m} B_j t^j</m></cell>
          <cell><m>k = \text{mult. of } 0</m> as root</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>A\, e^{\alpha t}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>B\, t^k e^{\alpha t}</m></cell>
          <cell><m>k = \text{mult. of } \alpha</m> as root</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="minor">
          <cell><m>A_1 \cos(\beta t) + A_2 \sin(\beta t)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>t^k\!\left(B_1 \cos(\beta t) + B_2 \sin(\beta t)\right)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>k = \text{mult. of } i\beta</m> as root</cell>
        </row>
        <row bottom="medium">
          <cell><m>e^{\alpha t}\!\left(A_1 \cos\beta t + A_2\sin\beta t\right)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>t^k e^{\alpha t}\!\left(B_1\cos\beta t + B_2\sin\beta t\right)</m></cell>
          <cell><m>k = \text{mult. of } \alpha{+}i\beta</m> as root</cell>
        </row>
      </tabular>
    </statement>
  </theorem>

  <remark xml:id="rem-guessing-method-scope">
    <title>Scope of the Guessing Method</title>
    <p>
      The method of undetermined coefficients works well when the forcing term
      <m>f(t)</m> is built from polynomials, exponentials, sines, cosines, or
      finite sums and products of these functions.  It is not a universal
      method for arbitrary right-hand sides such as <m>\ln t</m> or
      <m>\tan t</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      That scope restriction is useful to keep in mind: in this section we
      deliberately stay within the constant-coefficient setting where the
      forcing term suggests a manageable ansatz.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <remark xml:id="rem-resonance-explanation">
    <title>Why the Resonance Factor <m>t^k</m>?</title>
    <p>
      If the frequency of the right-hand side <m>f(t)</m> coincides with a
      root of the characteristic polynomial, the <q>naive</q> guess is itself
      a solution of the homogeneous ODE and therefore yields zero when
      substituted — it cannot absorb the forcing.
      Multiplying by <m>t^k</m> breaks the degeneracy: the adjusted
      guess is no longer in the homogeneous solution space.
    </p>
    <p>
      This phenomenon has a physical counterpart: when a driving force
      matches the natural frequency of an undamped oscillator the amplitude
      grows without bound (resonance).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <!-- ─── Worked example 1: polynomial RHS ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inhomogeneous-polynomial-rhs">
    <title>Inhomogeneous ODE — Polynomial Right-Hand Side</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y''(t) - 2y'(t) + y(t) = t^2 + 4</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Homogeneous solution.</em>
      We found earlier (<xref ref="ex-homogeneous-double-real-root"/>)
      that the characteristic polynomial is
      <m>P(\lambda) = (\lambda - 1)^2</m>
      and the general homogeneous solution is
      <me>
        y_h(t) = C_1\, e^t + C_2\, t\, e^t.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Particular solution.</em>
      The right-hand side is a polynomial of degree 2.
      Check for resonance: <m>P(0) = (0-1)^2 = 1 \neq 0</m>, so
      <m>\lambda = 0</m> is not a root and <m>k = 0</m>.
      Ansatz: <m>y_p(t) = B_0 + B_1 t + B_2 t^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compute the derivatives and substitute into the left-hand side:
      <md>
        <mrow>y_p'(t) \amp= B_1 + 2B_2 t,</mrow>
        <mrow>y_p''(t) \amp= 2B_2.</mrow>
      </md>
      <md>
        <mrow>
          y_p'' - 2y_p' + y_p
          \amp= 2B_2 - 2(B_1 + 2B_2 t) + (B_0 + B_1 t + B_2 t^2)
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= (B_0 - 2B_1 + 2B_2)
               + (-4B_2 + B_1)\, t
               + B_2\, t^2.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Equating coefficients with <m>f(t) = t^2 + 4 = 4 + 0\cdot t + 1\cdot t^2</m>:
      <md>
        <mrow>B_2 \amp= 1,</mrow>
        <mrow>-4B_2 + B_1 \amp= 0 \;\Rightarrow\; B_1 = 4,</mrow>
        <mrow>B_0 - 2B_1 + 2B_2 \amp= 4 \;\Rightarrow\; B_0 = 10.</mrow>
      </md>
      Thus <m>y_p(t) = 10 + 4t + t^2</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — General solution.</em>
      <me>
        y(t) = C_1\, e^t + C_2\, t\, e^t + t^2 + 4t + 10,
        \qquad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Worked example 2: resonance with exponential ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inhomogeneous-exponential-resonance">
    <title>Inhomogeneous ODE — Resonance with Exponential Forcing</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y''(t) + 6y'(t) + 9y(t) = 3e^{-3t}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Homogeneous solution.</em>
      <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 + 6\lambda + 9 = (\lambda + 3)^2</m>.
      Root <m>\lambda_0 = -3</m> has multiplicity 2, so
      <me>
        y_h(t) = C_1\, e^{-3t} + C_2\, t\, e^{-3t}.
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Particular solution.</em>
      The right-hand side is <m>f(t) = 3\, e^{-3t}</m>, so
      <m>\alpha = -3</m>.
      Since <m>\lambda = -3</m> is a root of <m>P</m> of multiplicity
      <m>k = 2</m>, we use the resonance ansatz
      <m>y_p(t) = B\, t^2 e^{-3t}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      Compute derivatives (using the product rule twice):
      <md>
        <mrow>y_p' \amp= B(2t - 3t^2) e^{-3t},</mrow>
        <mrow>y_p'' \amp= B(2 - 12t + 9t^2) e^{-3t}.</mrow>
      </md>
      Substituting into the ODE:
      <md>
        <mrow>
          y_p'' + 6y_p' + 9y_p
          \amp= B e^{-3t}\!\left[(2 - 12t + 9t^2)
               + 6(2t - 3t^2) + 9t^2\right]
        </mrow>
        <mrow>
          \amp= B e^{-3t}\!\left[2 + 0\cdot t + 0\cdot t^2\right]
            = 2B\, e^{-3t}.
        </mrow>
      </md>
      Setting <m>2B = 3</m> gives <m>B = \tfrac{3}{2}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — General solution.</em>
      <me>
        y(t) = C_1\, e^{-3t} + C_2\, t\, e^{-3t}
               + \tfrac{3}{2}\, t^2 e^{-3t},
        \qquad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <!-- ─── Worked example 3: complex exponential forcing ─── -->

  <example xml:id="ex-inhomogeneous-complex-exponential-forcing">
    <title>Inhomogeneous ODE — Exponentially Modulated Oscillation</title>
    <p>
      Find the general solution of
      <m>y''(t) + 2y'(t) + 5y(t) = e^{-3t}\sin(2t)</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 1 — Homogeneous solution.</em>
      <m>P(\lambda) = \lambda^2 + 2\lambda + 5</m>.
      Roots: <m>\lambda_{1,2} = -1 \pm 2i</m>.
      <me>
        y_h(t) = e^{-t}\!\left(C_1 \cos(2t) + C_2 \sin(2t)\right).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 2 — Particular solution.</em>
      The right-hand side has <m>\alpha = -3</m>, <m>\beta = 2</m>.
      The complex frequency <m>\alpha + i\beta = -3 + 2i</m> is
      <em>not</em> a root of <m>P</m> (since the roots are <m>-1 \pm 2i</m>),
      so <m>k = 0</m> and the ansatz is
      <me>
        y_p(t) = e^{-3t}\!\left(B_1 \cos(2t) + B_2 \sin(2t)\right).
      </me>
    </p>
    <p>
      Let <m>u(t) = B_1\cos(2t) + B_2\sin(2t)</m>.  After computing
      <m>y_p''+ 2y_p' + 5y_p</m> via the product rule and comparing
      coefficients with <m>e^{-3t}\sin(2t)</m>, one obtains the system
      <md>
        <mrow>-3 B_1 + 2 B_2 \amp= 0,</mrow>
        <mrow>-2 B_1 - 3 B_2 \amp= 1.</mrow>
      </md>
      Solving: <m>B_1 = -\tfrac{2}{13}</m>, <m>B_2 = -\tfrac{3}{13}</m>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <em>Step 3 — General solution.</em>
      <me>
        y(t) = e^{-t}\!\left(C_1\cos 2t + C_2\sin 2t\right)
               - \tfrac{e^{-3t}}{13}\!\left(2\cos 2t + 3\sin 2t\right),
        \quad C_1, C_2 \in \R.
      </me>
    </p>
  </example>

  <exercises xml:id="exercises-inhomogeneous-higher-order-check">
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-inhomogeneous-guess-polynomial">
      <title>Choose an Ansatz</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          For the ODE
          <me>
            y''-y=t,
          </me>
          which ansatz is appropriate for a particular solution?
        </p>
      </statement>
      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=At+B</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Correct. A polynomial right-hand side of degree <m>1</m> suggests a polynomial ansatz of degree <m>1</m>.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=Ae^t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. The forcing term is polynomial, not exponential.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=A\cos t + B\sin t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. Trigonometric ansatzes are used for sinusoidal forcing.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><m>y_p(t)=At\,e^t</m></p></statement>
          <feedback><p>Incorrect. There is no exponential forcing here.</p></feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>

    <exercise xml:id="exer-inhomogeneous-resonance-exp">
      <title>Find a Particular Solution in a Resonance Case</title>
      <statement>
        <p>
          Find one particular solution of
          <me>
            y''-2y'+y=e^t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </statement>
      <solution>
        <p>
          The homogeneous characteristic polynomial is
          <me>
            (\lambda-1)^2,
          </me>
          so <m>e^t</m> is already a homogeneous solution. We therefore use the resonance ansatz
          <me>
            y_p(t)=At^2e^t.
          </me>
          Substituting gives
          <me>
            y_p''-2y_p'+y_p=2Ae^t.
          </me>
          To match the right-hand side <m>e^t</m>, we choose <m>2A=1</m>, hence <m>A=\tfrac12</m>.
          Thus one particular solution is
          <me>
            y_p(t)=\tfrac12 t^2 e^t.
          </me>
        </p>
      </solution>
    </exercise>
  </exercises>

</subsection>
